#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73223c6b-80f8-5f93-9793-fb86cd7d5397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(15x+1)e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73223c6b-80f8-5f93-9793-fb86cd7d5397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(15x+1)e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325190} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e92480-dad9-5345-a383-6933341e3425" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b0f029e-226e-5aa8-b0f8-aab45fe31a03" :question/math-academy-id "q-332938" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=15{e}^{5x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=15{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}) & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c7ce128-42e5-5a5a-8fa2-0e897504ff9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b4a43e-7b1e-5f42-b952-3fc4f05b2e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xe^{-4x}-e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325193} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebd2d9b-c460-5687-b324-dfac8b95a5b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xe^{-4x}+e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07b4a43e-7b1e-5f42-b952-3fc4f05b2e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xe^{-4x}-e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325193} :answer-field/id #uuid "1310a78b-30d2-5e03-be94-9541f6c35e91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6698921c-b2b9-50d3-ac86-7d515f9b0b54" :question/math-academy-id "q-333147" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-4x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+4(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-4x+4x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x}+(\\beta +4\\beta ){e}^{x} & =8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x}+5\\beta {e}^{x} & =8{e}^{-4x}-5{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-4x}$ and ${e}^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =8\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =-5,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =8\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-4x}-{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba81125d-c905-5b57-af0b-715c31a4a497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12x+1)e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba81125d-c905-5b57-af0b-715c31a4a497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12x+1)e^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325197} :answer-field/id #uuid "47fe8f3a-0956-532c-8a2f-0ee306a9271f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "002b88e2-78e7-53d4-907a-a035eccfda11" :question/math-academy-id "q-333160" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=12{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=12{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}) & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "570160ff-c937-57e7-bd78-702f7f018e64" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc9f8eda-2a8e-595c-95b5-eb2407ed120e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{3x}-4e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325200} {:answer/id #uuid "7246a112-0d73-5a4b-84b2-951578d399f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3x}-4e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc9f8eda-2a8e-595c-95b5-eb2407ed120e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{3x}-4e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325200} :answer-field/id #uuid "a70c4757-9a08-554a-8c0c-6590910e6b28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60b4feb8-069e-5bff-99c3-ce2e18b5a210" :question/math-academy-id "q-333171" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha {e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x})-3(\\alpha {e}^{x}) & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-3)\\alpha {e}^{x} & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{x} & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-4{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-4{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4 & =1 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{3x}-4{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af1f1fb7-3e26-5624-ac07-0be869d2bd5e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c5f31a-829c-5ad8-8953-265e22200d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{4x}-2e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26c5f31a-829c-5ad8-8953-265e22200d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{4x}-2e^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325204} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a1b4a75-3b0a-5e46-9e77-59a44a257e8d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17e4a7b9-4c73-5615-b650-245db866cd3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-333178" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=6{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{4x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha {e}^{x}-4\\alpha {e}^{x} & =6{e}^{x} \\\\ -3\\alpha {e}^{x} & =6{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-2{e}^{x}.\n$$"}]}
